    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on a proposal for the new affordable housing mandate for the city council. We've got a limited number of units to allocate, and I'm trying to figure out what the most equitable way to distribute them would be. Should we do it through a lottery system, based strictly on income levels, or by prioritizing proximity to transit hubs?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "All three criteria you mentioned aim at equity, but the \"most equitable\" method depends on which specific social need your mandate is designed to address most urgently. If your primary goal is to reduce absolute poverty regardless of geography, a strict income threshold might be the most logical first filter, possibly followed by a lottery among the most eligible to prevent long waiting lists. However, if your focus is social inclusion and preventing urban segregation, prioritizing proximity to transit or using a full open lottery could better ensure access for all disadvantaged groups regardless of where they currently live.\n\nUltimately, the best approach might be a hybrid model that uses income as a hard qualifier to identify the most vulnerable, while using a transparent lottery or community selection for the remaining units to ensure fairness. I recommend you consult your legal and demographic experts to see which balance aligns best with the specific goals of the new city council mandate.",
